// Read-only lookups. Kelvora___get_approval must be permitted, otherwise its
// response is never recorded and no approval can be confirmed.
@id("allow-readonly-lookups")
permit (principal, action in [
    Drupe::Action::"Kelvora___get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"Ostrel___get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_failure_codes",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___list_workorders"
], resource);

// Closing: MAIN site only, and only if a completed Kelvora approval lookup in
// this session within the last hour confirmed that this work order at this
// site was GRANTED by a HUMAN.
@id("close-workorder-with-recent-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"MaintData___close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Kelvora___get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "GRANTED",
    output.approver_kind: "HUMAN"
  }
};
```

Everything else is denied by default, including `MaintData___approve_workorder`, so the agent cannot approve work orders itself.

The policies only trust the Kelvora lookup as proof of approval. Its output is a fixed enum (`decision`, `approver_kind`), so "GRANTED" and "HUMAN" are well-defined. Ostrel returns free strings with no declared vocabulary, so I left it as a plain lookup that cannot unlock a close.

I wrote the enum values as string literals, following the reference. If your engine requires entity-literal syntax for enum fields, those two patterns would need adjusting.

